const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"LR_E6A_Q001","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":false,"question_text":"The inspector of police, Chulbul Pandey is questioning five suspects – Sheroo, Santhosh, Bhayankar, Chola, and Bhala about a bank robbery. They made the following statements.\\nSheroo: Bhyankar robbed the bank.\\nSanthosh: Sheroo did not rob the bank.\\nBhayankar: Santhosh is telling the truth.\\nChola: Sheroo is telling the truth.\\nBhala: Exactly one of us is telling the truth. Chulbul Pandey just received evidence that Bhala is telling the truth. Then which of these statement is true?","options":{"A":"Sheroo robbed the bank.","B":"Bhayankar is telling the truth.","C":"Both Santhosh and Chola robbed the bank.","D":"Sheroo did not rob the bank."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Sheroo robbed the bank.","solution_text":"Since Bhala's statement (\\"exactly one of us is telling the truth\\") is confirmed true, and Bhala is one of the five, Bhala must be the ONLY truth-teller — the other four are all lying. Bhayankar lying makes Santhosh a liar; Santhosh lying (denying Sheroo robbed the bank) means Sheroo DID rob the bank; Chola lying means Sheroo is actually lying; Sheroo lying means Bhayankar did not rob the bank. So Sheroo robbed the bank.","entity_count":5,"variables":1,"constraints":5,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["truth_value_assignment","relational_decoding"],"question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"set_id":"LR_E6A_S2_4","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Truth-teller : A person who always speaks the truth.\\nLiar : A person who always lies.\\nAlternator : A person who alternates between truth and lie, in any order.","setup_solution":"For a single self-referential statement, checking whether each of the three types (Truth-teller, Liar, Alternator) COULD have said it means checking whether the statement's actual truth-value is compatible with that type's speaking rule. A Truth-teller can only utter statements that are true; a Liar can only utter statements that are false; an Alternator can utter either (since a lone statement doesn't reveal a pattern).","entity_count":3,"variables":1,"constraints":3,"case_splits":3,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":3,"information_load":"low","solving_methodologies":["self_referential_analysis","truth_value_assignment"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6A_Q002","question_text":"Who among the above three kinds of person can make the following statement – \\"I am not an alternator\\"?","options":{"A":"A truth-teller","B":"A liar","C":"An alternator","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Cannot be determined","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q003","question_text":"Who among the above three kinds of person can make the following statement – \\"I am not a truth-teller?\\"","options":{"A":"A truth-teller","B":"A truth-teller or a liar","C":"A liar or an alternator","D":"An alternator"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"An alternator","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q004","question_text":"Who among the above three kinds of persons can make the following statement – \\"I am a truth-teller?\\" I. A truth-teller II. A liar III. An alternator","options":{"A":"Only I","B":"Only I and III","C":"Only II and III","D":"Any one of I, II, III"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Any one of I, II, III","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75}]},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q005","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":false,"question_text":"On 'Kya-Kya' island, there are two tribes – Truth-tellers and Liars. Truth-tellers always speak the truth and Liars always lie. One day I met three Kya-Kya islanders A, B and C, and asked them 'Who among you is the truth-teller?' Following were their replies.\\nA : I am not a Liar.\\nB : C is not a Liar.\\nC : B is a Truth-teller. If it was known that exactly one person among the three was a Truth-teller and the other two were Liars, then who among them must be the Truth-teller?","options":{"A":"A","B":"B","C":"C","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"A","solution_text":"Testing each of A, B, C as the sole truth-teller against the statements, only A being the truth-teller (with B and C both liars) is internally consistent.","entity_count":3,"variables":1,"constraints":4,"case_splits":3,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"low","solving_methodologies":["case_elimination","truth_value_assignment"],"question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q006","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":false,"question_text":"One day I met three persons Anand, Bharat and Chandu each of whom belonged to a different tribe amongst Truth-tellers, Liars and Alternators. When asked to introduce themselves, each of them gave two replies, as given below. Also, it is known that the Truth-tellers speak truth and the Liars always lie and the Alternators alternate between truth and lie, in any order.\\nAnand : I am the Truth-teller. Bharat is the Liar.\\nBharat : I am not the Liar. Chandu is the Truth-teller.\\nChandu : I am not the Liar. Anand is not the Truth-teller. Who among these three persons is the Alternator?","options":{"A":"Anand","B":"Bharat","C":"Chandu","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Chandu","solution_text":"Testing all 6 possible type-assignments against the 6 statements, only one is consistent: Anand=Truth-teller, Bharat=Liar, Chandu=Alternator.","entity_count":3,"variables":1,"constraints":6,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","truth_value_assignment"],"question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q007","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":false,"question_text":"Three Players – Aalu, Kachaalu and Bhalu-were playing poker and suddenly started to quarrel among themselves - blaming each other for cheating. It was found out that at least one person among the three cheated. When they were asked who cheated, their replies were as follows.\\nAalu : I did not cheat. Kachaalu cheated.\\nKachaalu : I did not cheat. Both Aalu and Bhalu cheated.\\nBhalu : I did not cheat. Only Kachaalu did not cheat. If exactly one person among them always spoke truth, another always lied and the third alternated between the truth and lie, then which of the following statements can never be true in any case?","options":{"A":"Only Aalu and Bhalu cheated.","B":"Only Aalu and Bhalu did not cheat.","C":"Bhalu always spoke the truth.","D":"Bhalu alternated between truth and lie."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Bhalu always spoke the truth.","solution_text":"Testing all type-assignments and cheating-combinations together yields exactly two valid scenarios, and in both, Bhalu is always the Alternator — never the Truth-teller. So \\"Bhalu always spoke the truth\\" can never hold.","entity_count":3,"variables":2,"constraints":6,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":2,"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","case_elimination"],"question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"set_id":"LR_E6A_S8_10","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Three persons – Mohit, Nitin and Jayesh – gave one statement each. There is one person who speaks the truth, one who tells lies and another whose statement cannot be classified as either true or false. Following are the statements made by them:\\nMohit : I am not a liar.\\nJayesh : I am a Liar.\\nNitin : I neither speak the truth nor do I lie.","setup_solution":"Jayesh's statement (\\"I am a Liar\\") is self-contradictory whether Jayesh is a truth-teller or a liar, so Jayesh must be the person whose statement is unclassifiable. Nitin's statement can't be true (a truth-teller claiming to neither speak truth nor lie is self-contradictory) but is consistent as a lie, so Nitin is the liar, leaving Mohit as the truth-teller.","entity_count":3,"variables":1,"constraints":3,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["self_referential_analysis","case_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6A_Q008","question_text":"What is the name of the person whose statement can be classified as neither true nor as false?","options":{"A":"Mohit","B":"Jayesh","C":"Nitin","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Jayesh","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q009","question_text":"Who tells lies?","options":{"A":"Jayesh","B":"Mohit","C":"Nitin","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Nitin","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q010","question_text":"What is the nature of the statement given by Mohit?","options":{"A":"True","B":"False","C":"Cannot be classified as true or false","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"True","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60}]},{"set_id":"LR_E6A_S11_13","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Twelve coins were distributed among A, B and C, who always gave two replies to any question. When asked about the distribution of coins, following were their replies:\\nA : I got 4 coins. C did not get the least number of coins.\\nB : I got the least. A got the most.\\nC : A got the least. B got 4 coins. Each person received at least one coin. Also, it was known that one person among them always spoke the truth, another always tells lies and the third person alternates between the truth and lie, in any order.","setup_solution":"Since each question specifies a different coin distribution, the type-assignment (Truth-teller/Liar/Alternator) must be re-derived for each specific distribution given.","entity_count":3,"variables":1,"constraints":6,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","truth_value_assignment"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6A_Q011","question_text":"If each of them received equal number of coins, then who among them always tells lies?","options":{"A":"A","B":"B","C":"C","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"B","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q012","question_text":"If the coins were distributed as 3, 4 and 5, then who can never get three coins?","options":{"A":"A","B":"B","C":"C","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"C","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q013","question_text":"If the coins were distributed as 1, 2, and 9 then who got the least number of coins?","options":{"A":"A","B":"B","C":"C","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"B","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q014","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":false,"question_text":"Sameer, Sameep and Sumer participated in a quiz contest and each one of them received exactly one title amongst the Winner, the First Runner-up and the Second Runner-up. When asked, \\"Who among you three is the winner?\\", following were their replies:\\nSameer : I am the winner. Sameep is not the 1st Runner-up.\\nSameep : I am the winner. Sameer is the 2nd runner up.\\nSumer : I am the winner. Sameep is the 2nd runner-up. It is also known that one among them always tells the truth, one always lies and one alternates between the truth and lie (not necessarily in that order). Who can never be the first runner-up?","options":{"A":"Sameer","B":"Sameep","C":"Sumer","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Sameep","solution_text":"Testing all title/type combinations yields exactly three valid arrangements. Across all three, Sameep never holds the First Runner-up title (Sameer or Sumer always does instead).","entity_count":3,"variables":1,"constraints":6,"case_splits":3,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":3,"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","case_elimination"],"question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q015","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":false,"question_text":"Each person out of A, B and C had exactly one different title amongst the Good, the Bad and the Ugly. Also, each person always gave two answers to any question. Exactly one among them always spoke the truth, another always lied and the last person always alternates between truth and lie (in any order). When asked about their titles, following were their replies:\\nA : B is 'the Good'. I am 'the Ugly'.\\nB : C is 'the Bad'. A is not 'the Good'.\\nC : B is 'the Ugly'. A is not 'the Bad'. Which among the following choices has the names of the persons who had the title the Good, the Bad and the Ugly, respectively?","options":{"A":"A, B, C","B":"C, A, B","C":"B, C, A","D":"A, C, B"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"A, C, B","solution_text":"Testing all title/type combinations yields a unique consistent solution: A='the Good', C='the Bad', B='the Ugly', with A=Liar, B=Alternator, C=Truth-teller.","entity_count":3,"variables":1,"constraints":6,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","truth_value_assignment"],"question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q016","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":false,"question_text":"John, Johny and Janardan participated in a race and each won a different medal among Gold, Silver and Bronze, not necessarily in that order. Each person among them gives two replies to any question, one of which is true and the other is false (in any order). When asked about the details of the medals obtained by them, the following were their replies:\\nJohn : I won the Gold medal. Johny won the Bronze medal.\\nJohny : John won the Silver medal. I won the Gold medal.\\nJanardan : Johny won the Silver medal. I won the Gold medal. Which among the following is the correct order of the persons who won the Gold medal, the Silver medal and the Bronze medal respectively?","options":{"A":"John, Johny, Janardan","B":"Janardan, John, Johny","C":"Johny, Janardan, John","D":"Janardan, Johny, John"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Janardan, John, Johny","solution_text":"Since each person's two statements must include exactly one true and one false, testing all medal permutations yields a unique solution: John=Silver, Johny=Bronze, Janardan=Gold.","entity_count":3,"variables":1,"constraints":6,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["truth_value_assignment","constraint_elimination"],"question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"set_id":"LR_E6A_S17_19","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Each of the three persons – Ramu, Raman and Rajan- belonged to exactly one of different cities amongst Delhi, Mumbai and Chennai. Each person always gives two replies to any question asked. Out of these three, exactly one person always speaks the truth, other always lies and the another always alternates between truth and lie, in any order. When each was asked \\"Which city do you belong to?\\", the following were their replies:\\nRamu : I am from Delhi. Raman is from Mumbai.\\nRaman : I am from Delhi. Rajan is from Chennai.\\nRajan : Ramu is from Mumbai. Raman is from Delhi. Based on the above, answer the following questions.","setup_solution":"Testing all city/type combinations yields a unique solution: Ramu=Delhi (Truth-teller), Raman=Mumbai (Alternator), Rajan=Chennai (Liar).","entity_count":3,"variables":1,"constraints":6,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","truth_value_assignment"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6A_Q017","question_text":"Who among the three must be from Chennai?","options":{"A":"Ramu","B":"Raman","C":"Rajan","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Rajan","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q018","question_text":"Which city did Raman belong to?","options":{"A":"Delhi","B":"Mumbai","C":"Chennai","D":"Chennai or Delhi"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Mumbai","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q019","question_text":"If there are exactly two persons who always tell the truth, and the third person either always lies or alternates between truth and lie, then which of the following statements must be false?","options":{"A":"Rajan is not from Mumbai.","B":"Ramu is not from Delhi.","C":"Rajan is not from Chennai.","D":"Raman is from Delhi."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Rajan is not from Chennai.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E6A_S20_22","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"To save our solar system from the attacks of the ETs from other galaxies, the representatives of all nine planets gathered at the 'Galaxy Hall' for a meeting. While entering the Hall, each alien (representative of each planet) had to show his ID card to Mr. Gurkha-the gatekeeper. But three aliens-Eena, Meena and Deeka forgot their ID cards at the hotel. When asked, who represented which planet, the following were their replies.\\nEena : Deeka is from Mars. Meena is from Saturn.\\nMeena : Eena is from Jupiter. Deeka is not from Saturn.\\nDeeka : Meena is from Saturn. I am from Jupiter. It was known that exactly one amongst them belonged to the planet Jupiter, another belonged to the planet Saturn and the third belonged to the planet Mars. It was also known that each of them made at least one true statement.","setup_solution":"Testing all planet assignments with the \\"at least one true statement per person\\" constraint yields two valid solutions: (Eena=Jupiter, Meena=Saturn, Deeka=Mars) and (Eena=Mars, Meena=Saturn, Deeka=Jupiter).","entity_count":3,"variables":1,"constraints":6,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":2,"information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["case_elimination","truth_value_assignment"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6A_Q020","question_text":"Who must be from Saturn?","options":{"A":"Eena","B":"Meena","C":"Deeka","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Meena","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q021","question_text":"Which planet was Eena from?","options":{"A":"Mars","B":"Jupiter","C":"Saturn","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Cannot be determined","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q022","question_text":"Which of following statements can never be true?","options":{"A":"Each of Eena and Meena always spoke the truth.","B":"Each of Eena and Meena spoke one truth and one lie.","C":"Deeka always spoke the truth.","D":"The first statement given by each person was always false."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The first statement given by each person was always false.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E6A_S23_25","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"There are four persons – A, B, C and D – each of whom plays exactly one game from amongst Cricket, Football, Table-Tennis and Tennis. No game is played by two persons. Each person gives two replies to any question asked to them. At least one person among them always speaks the truth and at least one person always tells lies. There is at least one person who always alternates between the truth and lie in any order. When asked about the names of the persons and the respective games played by them, following were their replies:\\nA : I play Cricket. C plays Cricket.\\nB : I play Tennis. D plays Tennis.\\nC : A plays Table-Tennis. B plays Cricket.\\nD : C plays Football. I play Table-Tennis. It is also known that D plays Tennis and a definite arrangement can be obtained from the statements given by each person.","setup_solution":"Since D plays Tennis is given directly, testing all remaining game assignments and type combinations yields a unique solution: A=Table-Tennis (Liar), B=Cricket (Alternator), C=Football (Truth-teller), D=Tennis (Alternator).","entity_count":4,"variables":1,"constraints":9,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6A_Q023","question_text":"Who among the following plays Cricket?","options":{"A":"A","B":"B","C":"C","D":"D"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"B","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q024","question_text":"Who among the following always speaks the truth?","options":{"A":"A","B":"B","C":"C","D":"D"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"C","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q025","question_text":"Who are the two persons who always alternate between the truth and lie?","options":{"A":"A and D","B":"B and C","C":"D and B","D":"A and C"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"D and B","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E6A_S26_28","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"There are only four members of a family viz., A, B, C and D and there is only one couple among them. When asked about their relationships, following were their replies.\\nA : B is my son. D is my mother.\\nB : C is my wife. D is my father.\\nC : D is my mother-in-law. A is my daughter.\\nD : A is my granddaughter. B is my daughter-in-law. It was known that exactly one person among them always speaks the truth, one always tells lies and the other two persons always alternate between the truth and lie. All the relations mentioned exist only among them, if the relations exist at all. It is also known that there are exactly two males in the family.","setup_solution":"Testing the hypothesis that D is B's parent and B-C form the married couple, with A as B and C's daughter: setting D as male (B's father) makes all constraints consistent — B=Truth-teller, A=Liar, C and D=Alternators — and correctly gives exactly two males (D and B).","entity_count":4,"variables":2,"constraints":8,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["relational_decoding","type_assignment_testing"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6A_Q026","question_text":"Who always speaks the truth?","options":{"A":"A","B":"B","C":"C","D":"D"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"B","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q027","question_text":"How is B related to C?","options":{"A":"Father","B":"Mother","C":"Brother-in-law","D":"Husband"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Husband","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q028","question_text":"Which of the following statements must be true?","options":{"A":"A's grandmother alternates between the truth and lie.","B":"C's wife always speaks the truth.","C":"A's grandfather always speaks the truth.","D":"B's daughter always tells lies."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"B's daughter always tells lies.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E6A_S29_30","exercise":"exercise_6a","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Three Pundits – Dwivedi, Trivedi and Chaturvedi – sat in a row from left to right in some order, consisting of three seats – extreme left, centre and extreme right. Each person gives two replies to any question asked to them, at least one of which is true. There is exactly one person who always speaks the truth. When asked about their respective positions in the row, following were their replies:\\nDwivedi : I sat at the left end. Trivedi sat at the centre.\\nTrivedi : Dwivedi sat between me and Chaturvedi. I sat at the right end.\\nChaturvedi : I did not sit at the left end. Dwivedi did not sit at the right end. It is also known that a definite arrangement can be obtained by assessing their statements. What is the order in which they sat from left end to the right end of the row?","setup_solution":"Testing all seat permutations with \\"at least one true statement per person\\" and \\"exactly one person with both statements true\\" yields a unique solution: Dwivedi=Left, Chaturvedi=Centre, Trivedi=Right, with Chaturvedi as the sole truth-teller.","entity_count":3,"variables":1,"constraints":6,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination","truth_value_assignment"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6A_Q029","question_text":"What is the order in which they sat from left end to the right end of the row?","options":{"A":"Dwivedi, Trivedi, Chaturvedi","B":"Chaturvedi, Trivedi, Dwivedi","C":"Dwivedi, Chaturvedi, Trivedi","D":"Trivedi, Dwivedi, Chaturvedi"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Dwivedi, Chaturvedi, Trivedi","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E6A_Q030","question_text":"Which of the following can be inferred from the given information? I. Dwivedi's two statements are true. II. Trivedi's second statement is false. III. Chaturvedi's first statement is false.","options":{"A":"Only I","B":"Only I and III","C":"Only II and III","D":"None of the statements can be inferred"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"None of the statements can be inferred","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E6B_S1_3","exercise":"exercise_6b","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Alex, Bhavna and Charan are standing, not necessarily in that order, in different positions in a queue of 10 people. The numbering in the queue starts with position 1 at the beginning to position 10 at the end of the queue. When asked about their positions in the queue, they gave the following replies:\\nAlex : Charan is three places ahead of me. The number of people behind Bhavna is one more than the number of people ahead of Charan. If I interchange my position with Charan, I would be in position seven.\\nBhavna : I am fourth from the end of the queue. The sum of the numbers of our positions is a unit multiple of 5. I am exactly between Alex and Charan.\\nCharan : All of Alex's statements are false. If I interchange my position with Alex, I would be in position seven. Bhavna's first and second statements are true. It is known that exactly two of the three persons alternate between truth and lie in any order and Charan is one of them.","setup_solution":"Testing all position combinations with the constraint that exactly two people (including Charan) alternate and the third has a fixed type yields 5 valid solutions, all sharing Alex=position 7 (fixed) and Bhavna's statements all false (fixed), but Bhavna's own position (and hence her position relative to Alex) varies across the 5 solutions.","entity_count":3,"variables":1,"constraints":9,"case_splits":5,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":5,"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6B_Q001","question_text":"What is the position of Bhavna with respect to Alex?","options":{"A":"Immediately behind Alex.","B":"Two positions behind Alex.","C":"Three positions ahead of Alex.","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Cannot be determined","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q002","question_text":"Which of the following statements is true? I. Alex is in position seven. II. All the statements of Bhavna are false. III. Alex alternates between true and false statements in that order.","options":{"A":"Only II","B":"Only I and II","C":"Only I and III","D":"I, II and III"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Only I and II","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q003","question_text":"Which of the following additional conditions, if true, gives the exact positions of the three people?","options":{"A":"Bhavna is not behind Alex.","B":"Bhavna is immediately behind Alex.","C":"Charan is not ahead of Bhavna.","D":"None of the above"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Bhavna is immediately behind Alex.","question_operation":"identify_completing_statement","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150}]},{"set_id":"LR_E6B_S4_6","exercise":"exercise_6b","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"P, Q and R when asked a question give three statements as reply in the following manner. P – always replies in only one type of statements – truth or lie. Q – never replies in the same type of statements as P. R – is neither consistent in all his statement types nor alternates between the two types of statements. At the bus – stop, I asked: \\"Which bus goes to the airport?\\" Their replies were as follows:\\nP : Take the North – bound bus. The next bus arrives in 15 minutes. It takes 45 minutes to reach the airport.\\nQ : The north – bound bus comes from the airport. You have to wait for 20 minutes for the bus. It takes 30 minutes to reach the airport.\\nR : Take the South – bound bus. You have to wait for another quarter of an hour for the bus. It takes anywhere between 1 hour to 1 hour 30 minutes to reach the airport.","setup_solution":"Testing all combinations of actual bus/wait-time/travel-time facts against the type rules (P uniform, Q uniform-opposite-of-P, R mixed-non-alternating) yields a unique solution: the airport bus is South-bound, the wait is 20 minutes, and travel takes 30 minutes — making P a Liar (all false), Q a Truth-teller (all true), and R's pattern Truth-Lie-Lie. The total time from the bus-stop to the airport is the wait plus the travel time: 20 + 30 = 50 minutes.","entity_count":3,"variables":3,"constraints":9,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","truth_value_assignment"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6B_Q004","question_text":"Which of the following represents the reply that R gave?","options":{"A":"Truth – Truth – Lie","B":"Lie – Truth – Truth","C":"Lie – Lie –Truth","D":"Truth – Lie – Lie"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Truth – Lie – Lie","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q005","question_text":"Which of the following is definitely true? I. P always lies. II. Q always lies. III. The North – bound bus goes to the airport.","options":{"A":"Only I","B":"Only II","C":"Only I and II","D":"Only II and III"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Only I","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q006","question_text":"How long does it take before I reach the airport from the bus-stop?","options":{"A":"45 minutes","B":"50 minutes","C":"Between 1 hour and 1 hour 30 minutes","D":"30 minutes"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"50 minutes","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E6B_S7_11","exercise":"exercise_6b","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Three teenaged cousins (ages 13 – 19) Partha, Arjuna, and Sabyasachi when asked how old they are replied in the following manner.\\nPartha : Sabyasachi is two years younger than me. Arjuna lies about his age. Sabyasachi is between 14 and 18 years of age.\\nArjuna : I am 14 years old. Sabyasachi is 16 years old. Partha is less than 15 years of age.\\nSabyasachi : Partha is not the oldest. I am 19 years old. Partha's third statements is a lie. It is known that at any point of time exactly one among the three always speaks, the truth, one always lies and one always alternates between truth and lie in any order. Also, no two of them are of the same age.","setup_solution":"Testing all age combinations (13-19, distinct) with the truth-teller/liar/alternator constraint yields 20 valid solutions, falling into two families: one where Sabyasachi is always the Truth-teller (age 19, with Partha and Arjuna's exact ages and types varying), and one where Partha is the Truth-teller (age 18, Sabyasachi=16 as Liar, Arjuna=Alternator with varying age).","entity_count":3,"variables":1,"constraints":9,"case_splits":20,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":20,"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","case_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6B_Q007","question_text":"Which of the following must be true?","options":{"A":"Partha is the truth – teller.","B":"Arjuna is the truth – teller.","C":"Sabyasachi is the truth –teller.","D":"None of the above."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"None of the above.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q008","question_text":"If Arjuna is the alternator, then which of the following are true?","options":{"A":"Sabyasachi is 16 years old.","B":"Arjuna is 14 years old.","C":"Exactly one of (A) or (B)","D":"Both (A) and (B)"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Exactly one of (A) or (B)","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q009","question_text":"Sabyasachi will be 19 years of age when I. Partha is the liar. II. Arjuna is the alternator. III. Sabyasachi is the truth-teller.","options":{"A":"Only I","B":"I and either II or III only","C":"Only I and III","D":"Sabyasachi will never be 19 years of age."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Only I and III","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q010","question_text":"Which of the following statement(s) is/are definitely false?","options":{"A":"Partha's age is 13 years.","B":"Arjuna's age cannot be definitely determined.","C":"Partha's age is 14 years when Sabyasachi's age is 19.","D":"All the above statements are false."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Partha's age is 14 years when Sabyasachi's age is 19.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q011","question_text":"If Partha is the truth-teller then, which of the following is/are true? I. Partha's age is 18 years. II. Arjuna's second statement is true. III. Sabyasachi's third statement is false.","options":{"A":"Only II","B":"Only I and III","C":"Only II and III","D":"I, II and III"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"I, II and III","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E6B_S12_14","exercise":"exercise_6b","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"There are four executives A, B, C and D each working in a different organization amongst Microsoft, Oracle, Infosys and IBM, not necessarily in the same order. Each of them gives three replies to any question asked, which are alternately true or false, in any order. When asked to mention the name of the executive and the organization that he works for, they gave the following replies:\\nA : C works with Microsoft. D works with IBM. B works with Infosys.\\nB : A works with IBM. C works with Infosys. D works with Oracle.\\nC : D works with Microsoft. B works with Oracle. A works with Infosys.\\nD : B is not working with Infosys. A is working with IBM. C is not working with Microsoft. A definite arrangement can be obtained from the above data. Answer the questions based on the above data.","setup_solution":"Since everyone's three statements must follow a strict alternating true/false pattern, testing all organization permutations yields a unique solution: A=Microsoft, B=Oracle, C=Infosys, D=IBM.","entity_count":4,"variables":1,"constraints":12,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination","truth_value_assignment"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6B_Q012","question_text":"Who among the following works with Microsoft?","options":{"A":"A","B":"B","C":"C","D":"D"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"A","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q013","question_text":"B works with","options":{"A":"IBM","B":"Infosys","C":"Microsoft","D":"Oracle"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Oracle","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q014","question_text":"Which of the following statements must be false?","options":{"A":"D is working with IBM.","B":"The first statements made by A, B and C are false.","C":"C is working in Infosys.","D":"The second statements made by A, B and C are false."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The second statements made by A, B and C are false.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E6B_S15_17","exercise":"exercise_6b","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"On the eve of Army Day Parade, I met five Army men – Ranjeet, Ranmeet, Ranpreet, Randheer and Ranveer – each of whom had exactly one different rank from amongst Lieutenant, Captain, Colonel, Major and Brigadier, not necessarily in that order. Each of these persons always gave three replies to any question asked to them. Except one person, all the other four speak at least one true statement. Except one person, all the other four tell at least one lie. Exactly one person among them alternates between the truth and lie, in any order. When I asked them about their respective ranks, following were their replies:\\nRanjeet : Ranmeet is the Brigadier. Ranpreet is the Major. Ranveer is the Colonel.\\nRanmeet : Randheer is the Major. Ranjeet is the Captain. Ranpreet is the Lieutenant.\\nRanpreet : Ranjeet is the Colonel. Randheer is the Brigadier. Ranmeet is the Captain.\\nRandheer : Ranveer is the Lieutenant. Ranjeet is the Major. Ranpreet is the Captain.\\nRanveer : Ranmeet is the Colonel. Ranpreet is the Major. I am the Lieutenant. Only one definite arrangement exists based on their statements, which gives out their ranks.","setup_solution":"Testing all rank permutations with exactly one pure liar, one pure truth-teller, one alternator, and two mixed (non-alternating) speakers yields a unique solution: Ranjeet=Colonel (Alternator), Ranmeet=Captain (Liar), Ranpreet=Major (Truth-teller), Randheer=Brigadier (mixed, 1-true-2-false), Ranveer=Lieutenant (mixed, 2-true-1-false).","entity_count":5,"variables":1,"constraints":15,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6B_Q015","question_text":"Who among the following always speaks one truth and tells two lies but does not alternate between truth and lie?","options":{"A":"Ranjeet","B":"Ranpreet","C":"Randheer","D":"Ranmeet"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Randheer","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q016","question_text":"What is the rank of the person who alternates between the truth and lie, in any order?","options":{"A":"Brigadier","B":"Captain","C":"Major","D":"Colonel"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Colonel","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q017","question_text":"Match the following: Army men: (i) Ranjeet (ii) Ranmeet (iii) Ranpreet (iv) Randheer (v) Ranveer Ranks: (a) Lieutenant (b) Captain (c) Colonel (d) Major (e) Brigadier","options":{"A":"(i)–(a) (ii)–(b) (iii)–(c) (iv)–(d) (v)–(e)","B":"(i)–(b) (ii)–(c) (iii)–(e) (iv)–(a) (v)–(d)","C":"(i)–(c) (ii)–(e) (iii)–(d) (iv)–(b) (v)–(a)","D":"(i)–(c) (ii)–(b) (iii)–(d) (iv)–(e) (v)–(a)"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"(i)–(c) (ii)–(b) (iii)–(d) (iv)–(e) (v)–(a)","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E6B_S18_20","exercise":"exercise_6b","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"On True-Lies island, there are four categories of people namely Trues, who always speak the truth; Liars, who always lie; Altrues, whose first statement is true and make alternate true and false statements and Allies, whose first statement is false and make alternate false and true statements. A person from True Villa who visited the True Lies island has to make a telephone call to True Villa. He comes across a group of four people, where each member of the group belongs to a different category. He asks them about availability of telephone with them. Each person has a badge attached to his shirt with the name of a category to which he does not belong. The following are the statements made by each of them. The category on their badges is given in brackets. A (Trues): I. Allies have telephones. II. I have a telephone. B (Liars): I. I belong to Trues. II. I do not have a telephone. C (Altrues): I. I do not have a telephone. II. Only one of us has a telephone. D (Allies): I. The Liars have telephones. II. I belong to Altrues. In any category, either each member has a telephone or none has a telephone.","setup_solution":"Since each person's badge names a category they do NOT belong to, and each of the 4 people belongs to a different one of the 4 actual categories, testing all category and phone-possession combinations against each category's speaking rule yields a unique solution: A=Altrues, B=Trues, C=Allies, D=Liars, with only C (Allies) possessing a telephone.","entity_count":4,"variables":2,"constraints":8,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6B_Q018","question_text":"Who belong to the Trues category?","options":{"A":"A","B":"B","C":"C","D":"D"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"B","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q019","question_text":"Which of the following is true?","options":{"A":"B has a telephone.","B":"C has a telephone.","C":"Both B and C have telephones.","D":"Neither B nor C has a telephone."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"C has a telephone.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q020","question_text":"How many categories have a telephone?","options":{"A":"0","B":"1","C":"2","D":"3"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"1","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75}]},{"set_id":"LR_E6B_S21_23","exercise":"exercise_6b","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"In a family, there are four members Bingo, Tingo, Mingo and Pingo. Each member of that family belongs to one of the following Category – Truth-tellers (who always speaks truth), Liars (who always lies) and the alternators (who alternates among truth and lie, in any order). They made the following statements when they were asked about their professions.\\nBingo : I am a truth-teller. I am the Manager. Tingo is the Chartered Accountant.\\nMingo : I am a truth-teller. I am the Engineer. Professor is an alternator.\\nTingo : I am a truth-teller. I am the Professor. Pingo is the Engineer.\\nPingo : I am an alternator. I am the Professor. Bingo is a liar. It is also known that their professions are one among Manager, Engineer, Chartered Accountant and Professor (may not be in that order). And no two persons are of the same profession.","setup_solution":"Since types are not required to be all different here (multiple people could share a type), testing all profession and type combinations yields 10 valid base solutions with varying counts of truth-tellers, liars and alternators.","entity_count":4,"variables":2,"constraints":12,"case_splits":10,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":10,"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","case_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6B_Q021","question_text":"If Tingo is the Manager, then who is the Chartered Accountant?","options":{"A":"Bingo","B":"Mingo","C":"Pingo","D":"Either (A) or (B)"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Pingo","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q022","question_text":"If there is only one Liar and he is not Bingo, then who is the Engineer?","options":{"A":"Pingo","B":"Bingo","C":"Mingo","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Mingo","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q023","question_text":"From which of the given conditions, will we get a complete idea about them? I. There are exactly two liars. II. There are exactly two alternators. III. There is no truth teller.","options":{"A":"Only I","B":"Only II","C":"Any two of the above three","D":"Only III"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Any two of the above three","question_operation":"identify_completing_statement","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150}]},{"set_id":"LR_E6B_S24_26","exercise":"exercise_6b","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"John, James, Jack and Jeromy are compared with each other in terms of height, weight, age and wealth. Each of them gives three statements to any question, such that the three statements given by each person are alternately true and false, in any order. The first statement made by exactly two persons is false. When asked about the characteristic possessed by each of them, following were their replies:\\nJohn : I am the tallest. Jack is the heaviest. James is the richest.\\nJack : Jeromy is the shortest. I am the youngest. John is the second tallest.\\nJeromy : James is the lightest. Jack is the poorest. John is the eldest.\\nJames : I am the tallest. Jack is the lightest. Jeromy is the 2nd youngest. It is also known that no person gets the same rank (or position) in any two of the four comparisons and no comparison has two persons having the same rank.","setup_solution":"The only way the arrangement works is by assuming John and Jeromy's statement pattern is False-True-False, which forces Jack and James's pattern to be True-False-True. This gives the final rankings: Height – James(1), John(2), Jack(3), Jeromy(4); Weight – Jack(1), Jeromy(2), James(3), John(4); Age – John(1), Jack(2), Jeromy(3), James(4); Wealth – Jeromy(1), James(2), John(3), Jack(4).","entity_count":4,"variables":4,"constraints":14,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6B_Q024","question_text":"Who among the following is the 2nd heaviest?","options":{"A":"John","B":"Jack","C":"Jeromy","D":"James"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Jeromy","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q025","question_text":"Who is elder, wealthier, heavier, but shorter than James? I. Jack II. Jeromy III. John","options":{"A":"Only I","B":"Only II","C":"Only III","D":"II and III"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Only II","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q026","question_text":"How many persons weigh more than James?","options":{"A":"Zero","B":"One","C":"Two","D":"Three"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Two","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E6B_S27_30","exercise":"exercise_6b","type":"truth_teller_liar","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Each of the students A, B and C is from a different college among BIT, NIT and IIT majoring in a different stream among Civil, ECE and Mech, not necessarily in that order. Each of these colleges is located in a different city among Delhi, Mumbai and Goa and each of them has a different rank among one, six and twenty-four, not necessarily in that order. Each of A, B and C is one of a different category among truthteller, liar and inveigler, not necessarily in that order. (an inveigler speaks truth and lie in any order) The following are the statements made by A, B and C.\\nA: I am a truth teller. I am from BIT. B is studying in a college located in Delhi. C is majoring in Mech. My college is not ranked sixth.\\nB: I am from BIT. C is from IIT. A is majoring in Civil. IIT is ranked sixth. I am from NIT and it is not ranked first. I am an inveigler.\\nC: My college is ranked sixth. A studied in NIT which is located in Goa. I am not majoring in Mech. B is not majoring in ECE. IIT is ranked first. A is a truth teller.","setup_solution":"Testing all college/stream/city/rank/category combinations yields 4 valid solutions, all sharing the same college assignment (A=BIT, B=IIT, C=NIT), stream assignment (A=ECE, B=Civil, C=Mech), and category assignment (A=Truth-teller, B=Liar, C=Inveigler); only C's rank (24 or 1) and the exact city pairing vary across the 4 solutions.","entity_count":3,"variables":4,"constraints":16,"case_splits":4,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":4,"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["type_assignment_testing","constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E6B_Q027","question_text":"In which stream is B majoring?","options":{"A":"Civil","B":"Mech","C":"ECE","D":"Cannot be uniquely determined"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Civil","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q028","question_text":"In which stream is A majoring?","options":{"A":"Civil","B":"Mech","C":"ECE","D":"Cannot be uniquely determined"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"ECE","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q029","question_text":"What is the rank of the college located in Delhi?","options":{"A":"6","B":"24","C":"1","D":"Cannot be uniquely determined"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Cannot be uniquely determined","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E6B_Q030","question_text":"If the college in Goa is ranked 24, then what is the college located in Mumbai?","options":{"A":"NIT","B":"BIT","C":"IIT","D":"Cannot be uniquely determined"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"NIT","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150}]}]`);export{e as default};
